is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r7
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting OSPF (ospfd) on R7. Mechanism: When ospfd on R7 stops, its LSAs for the “R7 region” (its loopback 17.x.x.x, the transit/stub networks 67.0.0.0/8 on r7-eth0 and 70.0.0.0/8 on r7-eth1) are withdrawn from the area; upstream routers like R1 then lose those routes from their OSPF table, matching the red-screen alarm. Localization: Device R7, OSPF adjacency to R6 over interface eth0 (Y: 67.1.1.2) and advertising connected 70.0.0.0/8 on eth1 (AA). Current evidence shows recovery: R1 again has OSPF routes to 17/8, 67/8, and 70/8 (ages ~30–60s). R7’s OSPF neighbor is Full with 16.1.4.1 (R6) over 67.1.1.2 with uptime ~1m, and R6 sees 67.1.1.2 as Full/DR, indicating a recent restart. On R7, ospfd and zebra are running under watchfrr (ps output), even though “systemctl status frr” misleadingly reports inactive; the daemons are not managed by systemd in this container. Root cause: R7’s ospfd process crashed/restarted recently, temporarily withdrawing its LSAs. Corrective fix: No immediate network change required—the region is back up. To prevent recurrence and operator confusion, keep FRR under watchfrr supervision (as currently running) and ignore the systemctl status for FRR in this environment, or properly register and enable the frr.service with restart=always. Also review FRR logs (/var/log/frr) on R7 for crash signatures and enable watchdog alerts; if crashes persist, upgrade FRR or adjust OSPF config accordingly.
=== END ===